WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.213
Then enforcement.

2
00:00:01.213 --> 00:00:04.713
The pilot runs on rings zero
and one for at least two weeks,

3
00:00:04.713 --> 00:00:08.661
with network protection and
controlled folder access in audit;

4
00:00:08.661 --> 00:00:12.087
that length is the program's
risk-based recommendation.

5
00:00:12.087 --> 00:00:14.916
Read the audit events
every week and add allowed

6
00:00:14.916 --> 00:00:18.663
applications for controlled
folder access before you enforce.

7
00:00:18.663 --> 00:00:23.063
Then turn each setting to block in
the Intune policy, ring by ring.

8
00:00:23.063 --> 00:00:27.672
Ring zero, the implementing team, exits
when the audit events are reviewed,

9
00:00:27.672 --> 00:00:31.300
the test site is blocked and no
one has called the help desk.

10
00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:34.034
Ring one, the pilot
group, after two weeks,

11
00:00:34.034 --> 00:00:39.087
when every audited line-of-business
block has an allow entry or a decision.

12
00:00:39.087 --> 00:00:42.905
Ring two, early adopters, after
one week, with help-desk calls

13
00:00:42.905 --> 00:00:47.112
tagged Defender block under the
threshold set before the change.

14
00:00:47.112 --> 00:00:49.064
Ring three, everyone in scope,

15
00:00:49.064 --> 00:00:53.050
when post-change validation
passes on a sample at each site.

16
00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:57.766
The backout triggers are a blocked
critical application with no allow path,

17
00:00:57.766 --> 00:01:00.162
or help-desk volume over the threshold.

18
00:01:00.162 --> 00:01:03.500
Rollback sets the mode back
to audit in the same policy.

19
00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:06.132
Microsoft Learn says tamper protection is

20
00:01:06.132 --> 00:01:08.830
turned off only from
the console that set it.
